        <td class="num">$287.75</td>
        <td class="num">$244.99</td>
        <td class="num">$49,900</td>
      </tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>

  <!-- Condition premium sub-section -->
  <h3 style="margin-top:32px; margin-bottom:16px; font-size:1.05rem;">New vs. Used Price Premium</h3>
  <div class="chart-row full">
    <div class="chart-card">
      <h3>Median Price by Condition & Brand</h3>
      <div class="chart-wrap"><canvas id="chartCondPremium" height="240"></canvas></div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="insight">
    <strong>Value retention insight:</strong> Apple shows a clear 17.2% new-over-used premium (median $590 vs. $503), making "Used" Apple watches the strongest value play. Garmin's near-zero premium (-0.5%) means buying new Garmin on the secondary market carries almost no markup over used — remarkable given typical depreciation curves. Samsung's "Open Box" median ($675) substantially exceeds its "New" ($525), likely driven by bundled or premium-edition open-box listings.
  </div>
</div>

<!-- SECTION 3: Geographic Pricing Arbitrage -->
<div class="section">
  <div class="section-header">
    <div class="section-num">3</div>
    <h2>Geographic Pricing Arbitrage</h2>
  </div>
  <p class="section-intro">This is where real margin opportunities surface. Country-level median prices vary by as much as $399 for a single brand. The UK is consistently cheap for Apple but expensive for Garmin and Samsung — a striking divergence that creates brand-specific sourcing strategies.</p>

